
  [1mB. Materialized — notifications are records appended when a trigger fires.[0m
  A processing pass walks the change log and the clock, and writes notification rows with a lifecycle (unread → read →
  dismissed).
  [3mWins:[0m real history, straightforward unread counts, and it's the shape a future server-backed version would need
  anyway.
  [3mCosts:[0m they go stale. Delete a task and you have a dangling notification; un-complete one and the old alert is now
  wrong. Every trigger pass risks duplicates, so you end up writing dedupe logic regardless — which is most of approach
  C.

  [1mC. Hybrid — pure rules produce candidates with deterministic IDs; a reconciler owns lifecycle.[0m [3m(recommended)[0m
  Rules stay pure functions returning candidate notifications, each with an ID derived from its cause —
  [38;5;153m${taskId}:${ruleId}:${occurrence}[39m. A reconciler diffs that candidate set against stored lifecycle state: new IDs
  appear as unread, IDs that vanish are dropped, IDs you've dismissed stay suppressed.
  [3mWins:[0m dedupe is structural rather than defensive — the same cause always yields the same ID, so re-running the pass is
  idempotent and firing on every tick is safe. You keep A's self-healing [3mand[0m B's persistent read/dismiss state. The
  engine stays pure and unit-testable with a fake clock; only the reconciler touches storage.
  [3mCosts:[0m one extra concept (the reconciler), and the ID scheme has to be designed with care — "occurrence" is what stops
  a daily-reminder rule from collapsing all its firings into one.

  [1mI recommend C.[0m A and B each solve half the problem and converge on C the moment you add the other half; you'd pay for
  it eventually and more expensively. C is also the seam I mentioned for multi-user later — swapping local storage for a
  server changes the reconciler and nothing else.
